+
+# Keep post revisions on every edit
+
+## Context
+
+Saving the edit form overwrote a post for good. Authors pasted the wrong
+draft over a published post and had no way back. Editorial asked for a
+history of earlier versions of each post and a one-click restore.
+
+## Decision
+
+- Every successful save of an existing post first copies the title, excerpt
+  and body it is about to replace into `post_revisions`. Creating a post
+  keeps nothing, and neither does a save that is refused.
+- The copy and the overwrite run in one transaction, in `Post.revise()`,
+  which reads the current row inside that transaction. It is the only path
+  that changes a post's content.
+- Restoring a revision is an edit: it goes through `Post.revise()` too, so
+  the version the post had just before the restore is kept as a new
+  revision and the restored one stays in the list.
+- The history is as private as the edit form. Both revision routes sit
+  behind the `auth` middleware and authorize as `update` on the post, so
+  `PostPolicy` stays the single statement of who may change a post.
+- A revision is looked up by its id *and* the post's id. A revision of
+  another post answers 404, even for an author who owns both.
+- Deleting a post deletes its revisions in the same transaction. The
+  foreign key also declares `ON DELETE CASCADE`, but SQLite enforces it
+  only with `foreign_keys` switched on, so the code does not rely on it.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A post's content must never be written with `Post.update()` directly, or
+  the replaced version is lost.
+- A save that changes nothing still keeps a revision, and revisions are
+  never pruned: the table grows with every save. Pruning and side-by-side
+  comparison are out of scope for now.
+- Revisions cover title, excerpt and body only; a new content column on
+  `posts` needs a matching column on `post_revisions`.
